div1.note:
Abby's son, Arthur,
                    joined the navy and became second assistant engineer on the steamer "Ossipee";
                    see Whitman's address book (The Library of Congress #109).

John Burroughs's article;
                    see Whitman's letter from November 23, 1866
                    .

Construction of the
                    Washington Monument began in 1848; construction was abandoned from 1855 to 1877;
                    and finally it was completed in 1884.

This draft letter is
                    endorsed, "letter to Mr. Morley | reach'd London | probably New Year's | day";
                    "went by steamer Dec 19 | reach'd London | New Year's."

This draft letter is
                    endorsed, "Robt Buchanan | (must have gone 17th by Scotia | from N.Y.)";
                    "Buchanan | May 16 '76."

note.p:
This letter's envelope bears the address, "P B Marston | 20 Ladbroke Grove
                        Road | London W England." It is postmarked: "Camden | Sep | 7 | N.J.;
                        Philad'a | Sep | 8 | Paid."

div1.note:
The first letter is lost;
                    the extant correspondence begins with Tennyson's July
                        12, 1871 letter to Whitman.

note.p:
This appears to be a note written while Whitman was still in Washington;
                        therefore, the year 1873 seems plausible, since before his illness Whitman
                        would have gone for the books himself.

div1.note:
The Galaxy published O'Connor's "The Ballad of Sir Ball" in March of 1868;
                    see Whitman's May 18, 1868, letter to Messrs.
                    Sheldon and Company.

